<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Armstrong Custer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Armstrong Custer was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army officer who rose to fame as a cavalry commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. After the war he led the 7th Cavalry on the Great Plains, and his expedition of 1874 confirmed the presence of gold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>. He and over 260 of his men were killed by Lakota and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> warriors at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn">Battle of the Little Bighorn</a> in June 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer.jpg" alt="George Armstrong Custer" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer is Mathew Brady (died 1896) and his field staff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Custer_Bvt_MG_Geo_A_1865_LC-BH831-365-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
